<onlyinclude>A '''chiplet''' is a small, separately manufactured chip that works with other chips inside one package. Instead of making every function on one large die, designers can combine dies for computing, memory and connections to other devices.

== Why use chiplets? ==
Smaller dies may be easier to manufacture successfully and can let designers reuse components or mix fabrication technologies. But connecting dies also consumes energy, space and time, and the package must be assembled and tested carefully.

== Are chiplets the same as 3D chips? ==
No. Chiplets describe the components and how a system is divided. They can sit beside each other or be stacked. A stacked package is not automatically a chiplet architecture.

